Providers must ensure that training, validation and testing data sets are subject to data governance and management practices appropriate for the intended purpose of the high-risk AI system, whenever such data sets are used (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57). Those practices must concern in particular:

- the relevant design choices (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57);
- data collection processes and the origin of the data, and, for personal data, the original purpose of the data collection (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57);
- relevant data-preparation processing operations, such as annotation, labelling, cleaning, updating, enrichment and aggregation (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57);
- the formulation of assumptions, in particular about the information the data are supposed to measure and represent (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57);
- an assessment of the availability, quantity and suitability of the data sets that are needed (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57);
- examination for possible biases that are likely to affect health and safety, have a negative impact on fundamental rights, or lead to discrimination prohibited under Union law, especially where data outputs influence inputs for future operations (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57);
- appropriate measures to detect, prevent and mitigate possible biases identified through that examination (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57); and
- the identification of relevant data gaps or shortcomings that prevent compliance with the Regulation, and how those gaps and shortcomings can be addressed (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 57).

The documents also state that high-quality training, validation and testing data sets require appropriate data governance and management practices, and that such data sets should be relevant, sufficiently representative, and, to the best extent possible, free of errors and complete in view of the intended purpose (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 19). For personal data, data governance and management practices should include transparency about the original purpose of the data collection (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 19). The documents further say that these data-governance requirements can be complied with by using third parties that offer certified compliance services, including verification of data governance, data set integrity, and data training, validation and testing practices (eu-ai-act.pdf, p. 19).
